<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Persistence.Tests
    Unit and service-level tests for the Persistence service of the PowerFramework .NET 10
    decomposition. Persistence is the only service that generates or executes SQL and the only
    one holding a storage provider, so this project is where the retrieval / validation / update
    triple, the two paging rewriters, the SQLite URI grammar and the optimistic-concurrency
    contract are to be held to legacy parity.

    WHAT IS HERE TODAY, AND HOW DEEPLY. Stated because "is where X is held to parity" reads as a
    claim about existing coverage, so the claim is reported as measured rather than as intended.
    Every figure below comes from the Cobertura report this project emits, counting the sequence
    points that report calls lines; an earlier revision of this comment described a much earlier
    state of the tree and is superseded in every particular.

      BUILD AND TEST STATUS, MEASURED: this project builds in Release with zero warnings and zero
      errors, and its whole suite passes - 2439 tests across 33 of its 34 files, the thirty-fourth
      being GlobalUsings.cs, with no failure and no skip. The application project it references
      carries Program.cs and produces an entry point, so the CS5001 an earlier revision reported is
      no longer reachable.

      COVERAGE, MEASURED: PowerFramework.Persistence sits at 81.7 percent line coverage, which
      clears the 80 percent per-service floor constraint C-H requires. Every production type in
      the application project apart from the three EF-generated migration files is named by at
      least one test file here, including the internal ItemStatusMachine in Buffers/ItemStatus.cs
      and the internal PagedUniqueIndexColumnValidator in Sql/Paging/IPagingRewriter.cs, neither of
      which has a file of its own.

      DRIVEN THOROUGHLY: the three DataWindow buffers and the item-status machine; both transfer
      codecs, changeset and full state, including their receive and transfer paths; the SELECT
      statement model and its compound-select behaviour; ClauseModifier; the paged unique-index
      column validator; UpdateWhereBuilder; IdentityColumnResolver; ConflictDetector; SqlRedactor;
      DbErrorData; CompanyEntity; TransactionData and TransactionPool; SqlTaskBase and SqlQueryTask
      with all three task proxies; the Command, Transaction and Update gRPC services; the health
      and ping endpoints; and the composition root.

      BOTH PAGING REWRITERS ARE COVERED, contrary to an earlier revision of this comment.
      SqlServerPagingRewriterTests.cs carries 27 cases across the three SQL Server strategies and
      PagingRewriterByteExactTests.cs carries 3 more, together with the dispatcher's
      DBT_MSSQL / DBT_ORACLE selection and its E_NO_IMPLEMENTATION arm for anything else. These are
      pure string transforms, so they need no database instance of either kind - which is exactly
      why docs/PARITY.md section 6.2 treats them as byte-exact matrices.

      REACHED ONLY SHALLOWLY, WITH THE MEASURED RATE, SO THE NEXT READER KNOWS WHERE TO WORK:
      Grpc/QueryService at 5.7 percent, whose chunked server-streaming members are entered only by
      the composition-root registration test; Tasks/SqlUpdateTask at 24.1 percent, driven today
      through its proxy rather than directly; Data/SqliteConnectionFactory at 38.0 percent, where
      the pure URI and option surface is exercised but the members that actually open a connection
      are not; Tasks/TaskProxies/SqlTaskProxyBase at 58.0 percent; and
      Configuration/PersistenceOptions at 59.6 percent, whose unreached half is its validation
      failure arms.

      NOT EXERCISED AT ALL, AND EACH FOR A STATED REASON: Data/PowerFrameworkDbContext together
      with its design-time factory, and the three files under Data/Migrations. The migrations are
      EF-generated and are meaningful only when applied against a database, so a unit test over
      them would assert the generator's output rather than this service's behaviour; the schema
      they create is instead pinned by CompanyEntityTests against the single DDL the legacy tree
      publishes.

    THIS PROJECT IS THE MEASURED UNIT FOR THE COVERAGE GATE, AND THE GATE IS NOW ENFORCED.
    Constraint C-H requires 80 percent line coverage per in-scope service, evaluated per service
    so that one service's coverage cannot mask another's. The Cobertura report this project emits is
    the artifact that gate reads, which is why coverlet.collector below is load bearing and must
    never be trimmed as an unused reference.

    .github/workflows/ci.yml enforces it, in this project's own matrix leg, and reads the report's
    top level line rate directly. That rate is the number for this service alone because the
    repository root coverage.runsettings scopes the report to the four service assemblies: without
    it the report would also cover the shared libraries and the generated protobuf stubs in
    PowerFramework.Contracts, and its top level rate would read 28.11 percent for this service
    rather than 81.74. The gate additionally asserts that the report contains EXACTLY ONE package
    and that it is PowerFramework.Persistence, so a filter that silently stopped matching fails the
    leg instead of quietly changing the subject of the measurement.

    Two consequences worth knowing before changing anything here. Removing or renaming
    coverlet.collector, or dropping the settings file from the test command, makes the leg fail with
    a structural error rather than a coverage number. And the leg does not fail fast against its
    three siblings, so a shortfall here is reported alongside their verdicts rather than hiding them.

    HAND-AUTHORED ON PURPOSE, NOT TEMPLATED.
    `dotnet new xunit3` does not exist in SDK 10.0.302 (it exits 103 and suggests searching
    NuGet.org), and `dotnet new xunit` scaffolds the xunit v2 line with an older test SDK and an
    older coverage collector. Neither can produce this file, so it is written by hand, and its
    shape mirrors the Gateway sibling element for element because the four service test manifests
    must be structurally identical: versionless PackageReference elements throughout, with
    PrivateAssets and IncludeAssets metadata on exactly the two build-time tooling packages
    (xunit.runner.visualstudio and coverlet.collector) and on neither of the three library ones.
    Gateway is the reference; DataServices and Security carry the same pairing. If this file ever
    disagrees with those three, this file is the one that is wrong.

    NOTHING INHERITED IS RESTATED HERE.
    TargetFramework (net10.0), LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, TreatWarningsAsErrors,
    EnableNETAnalyzers, AnalysisLevel and ManagePackageVersionsCentrally all arrive from the
    repository-root Directory.Build.props by MSBuild directory walk-up; package versions arrive
    from the repository-root Directory.Packages.props. Duplicating an inherited property here is
    how the four services silently drift apart later, so this file declares only what is genuinely
    local to it. Warnings are errors in test code too.

    NO GLOBAL USING ITEMS HERE.
    The sibling GlobalUsings.cs file is the single owner of this assembly's `global using`
    declarations, and xunit.v3 injects no MSBuild Using items of its own, so nothing is missing.
    An MSBuild Using element here would compile (Roslyn silently deduplicates an identical global
    using arriving from two sources, verified: zero warnings, zero errors), but it would split one
    concern across two mechanisms and leave the four service test projects inconsistent, since the
    Gateway test folder carries no GlobalUsings.cs at all. One owner, discoverable from the file
    name, is the point.

    NO InternalsVisibleTo HERE EITHER.
    That attribute points the other way: the application project grants this assembly access, so
    sibling test files CAN drive internal members, and all three uses an earlier revision listed as
    future are current fact. Tests here drive the internal SelectStatementModel and
    ItemStatusMachine; SqlServerPagingRewriterTests.cs reaches the internal pfwPagedSQL_Tbl sentinel
    inside SqlServerPagingRewriter; and WebApplicationFactory binds to the implicitly generated
    internal Program class with no `public partial` shim, which is what CompositionRootTests.cs,
    HealthEndpointsTests.cs and PingEndpointsTests.cs each depend on.
    Data/SqliteConnectionFactory, which an earlier revision recorded as missing from the tree, is
    present, and its URI and option composition is driven from here; what is not driven is the
    subset of its members that open a connection.

    Validated command, from AAP section 0.8.3 and the attached environment's setup instructions,
    which must keep working with no extra argument (constraints C-I and C-L). The literal command
    line cannot be quoted here because a double hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment and would
    make this project file malformed, so the collector option is named rather than spelled:

        cd services/persistence-service
        dotnet restore
        dotnet build -c Release
        dotnet test, requesting the "XPlat Code Coverage" data collector

    Expected and not a defect: bare `dotnet test` builds Debug even immediately after
    `dotnet build -c Release`. The documented command is retained verbatim; the release-configuration
    variant, which adds `-c Release` alongside the same collector option, is recorded in
    docs/BUILD.md.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      MANDATORY, not a style choice. A v3 test assembly is self-executing: xunit.v3 generates its
      entry point with the Xunit.v3.MSBuildTasks.XunitGenerateEntryPoint task and then, in its
      _XunitValidateBuild target, raises a hard MSBuild error unless OutputType is Exe and an app
      host is produced. Do not rely on Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk quietly coercing this: that package
      does force Exe for .NET Core test projects, but it is a coincidence of the current package
      set rather than a contract, and the xunit error fires the moment the coercion goes away.
      Declaring it here keeps the requirement visible and locally true.
    -->
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!--
      Stated explicitly rather than inferred from the file name, because the application project's
      InternalsVisibleTo names this assembly by string. A rename that silently changed the
      assembly identity would revoke internal access and break compilation of the sibling tests.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>PowerFramework.Persistence.Tests</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>PowerFramework.Persistence.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <!-- A test project is never a shipped package. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly five packages, every one versionless.
    Central Package Management is switched on at the repository root, so a Version attribute here
    is a build error and the single source of truth stays Directory.Packages.props
    (xunit.v3 3.2.2, xunit.runner.visualstudio 3.1.5, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 18.8.1,
    coverlet.collector 10.0.1, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing 10.0.10). Each is load-bearing:

      xunit.v3                        the test framework itself.
      xunit.runner.visualstudio       the discovery adapter. Without it the standard test command
                                      discovers nothing at all and reports a vacuous pass.
      Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk          the test host that `dotnet test` drives.
      coverlet.collector              emits coverage.cobertura.xml, the exact artifact the
                                      80 percent per-service line-coverage gate is measured from.
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing  supplies WebApplicationFactory for the service-level
                                      endpoint and gRPC tests that sit alongside the unit tests.
                                      Its nuspec declares a framework reference to
                                      Microsoft.AspNetCore.App, which is why this project can stay
                                      on Microsoft.NET.Sdk instead of Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web.

    Nothing else may be added. In particular: no Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens, because
    Security is the sole token issuer and Persistence never mints (C-G); no
    Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing, because the transaction-pool clock double is
    hand-authored against the BCL TimeProvider and that package is deliberately absent from
    Directory.Packages.props; no assertion, mocking or container library; and no SQL Server or
    Oracle client, because both DBMS behaviours are preserved as pure string paging rewriters that
    need no database instance. A package that is not pinned centrally cannot restore.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Test stack">
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />

    <!--
      THE TWO TOOLING PACKAGES CARRY ASSET METADATA; THE THREE LIBRARY PACKAGES DO NOT.
      The split is not cosmetic and it matches the Gateway, DataServices and Security siblings
      element for element, which is the point - four structurally identical service test manifests
      mean a reader who has understood one has understood all four.

      PrivateAssets=all stops these two flowing onward to anything that references this assembly.
      They are build-time tooling, not part of any API surface, so propagating them would put test
      infrastructure into an unrelated project's dependency closure.

      IncludeAssets deliberately RETAINS `build`, and dropping it would be the damaging mistake
      here: both packages deliver their registration through MSBuild targets in their `build`
      assets, so a narrower list silently disables discovery and collection. The failure mode is
      the worst kind - the test command still exits successfully while finding nothing, and the
      coverage gate then reads a Cobertura report with no data, which looks like success.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>

    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />

    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>

    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly one project reference: this service's own application project, and nothing else.
    That is constraint C-A (decompose, do not collapse) in mechanical form. No reference reaches
    PowerFramework.Gateway, PowerFramework.DataServices or PowerFramework.Security, and no
    shared/ project is referenced directly: the shared kernel, diagnostics, containers and the
    contracts assembly all arrive transitively through the application project, as do
    Microsoft.Data.Sqlite and the EF Core SQLite provider. Persistence therefore restores, builds
    and tests from a clean checkout with no knowledge of any sibling service (C-I).
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Subject under test">
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PowerFramework.Persistence\PowerFramework.Persistence.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
